---

<MarketingPage
  title="Why I'm Building Shiba — An Open-Source Devin Cloud Alternative"
  description="The personal reason behind Shiba: bring your own harness and models, route each task to the right model, run it on your own Cloudflare account, and keep errors honest."
  path="/why-shiba/"
  type="article"
  publishedTime="2026-09-26T00:00:00Z"
  modifiedTime="2026-09-26T00:00:00Z"
  schema={articleSchema}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Why Shiba', path: '/why-shiba/' },
  ]}
>
  <!-- HERO MANIFESTO -->
  <section class="shell hero">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--navy)] mb-6 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
      <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-[var(--navy)] animate-pulse"></span>
      <span>FOUNDER MANIFESTO · SEPTEMBER 2026</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="manifesto-title text-[var(--ink)]">
      Every developer deserves an <span class="text-[var(--navy)]">AI software engineer</span> they actually own.
    </h1>
    <p class="lead text-[var(--ink-2)]">
      I am building <strong>Shiba</strong> as an open-source alternative to Devin Cloud: an approval-gated coding coworker in <em>your own Cloudflare account</em>, where you bring the harness and the models, route each task to the right one, and can see the work — and its failures — for yourself.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button" href="/waitlist/">
        <span>Join the Waitlist &amp; Contribute</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="/docs/getting-started/">
        <span>Read Quickstart Guide</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>GitHub Source ↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell story-section" aria-labelledby="origin-title">
    <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">00 / Why I Started</span></div>
    <div class="prose-body">
      <h2 id="origin-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">I loved sending work to the cloud. I did not love losing the thread.</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">The useful part of Devin Cloud, for me, was handing off a coding task and letting it continue in the background while I did something else. That is the experience I want to keep.</p>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">But three things kept bothering me. First, the agent was locked to one model for every kind of task. A slow, heavyweight model is fine for a big refactor, but it's overkill — and wasteful — for a small frontend tweak. I wanted to send small tasks to a fast, cheap model and route the heavy ones to something stronger: Opus for the hard work, a lighter model when speed matters more. <strong>Task-aware model routing, with one shared brain across harnesses and modes</strong> — that was missing, so I decided to build it.</p>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">Second, real work needs real access. When an agent needs to run the Wrangler CLI or touch my Cloudflare account, I don't want it holding my personal credentials — I want to give it its own dedicated agent account with least-privilege access. None of the tools I was paying for gave me that, so I created that account myself and started building around it.</p>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">And third: sometimes a task would run into an infrastructure error. When background work stops, I need to know what actually happened — whether the agent changed files, whether tests ran, and what still needs a human decision. A cheerful "done" message is not a substitute for that evidence.</p>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">So I am building Shiba around a narrower promise: review the exact request before a sandbox starts, pick the harness and model that fit the task, keep the run in a workspace I control on a plan I already pay for, and show progress, errors, and diffs without pretending an interrupted task succeeded. It is an open-source Devin Cloud alternative, not a claim of affiliation or a promise that infrastructure will never fail.</p>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">The name? A Shiba Inu is a famously loyal Japanese dog — small, independent, and stubbornly its own. That felt right: a software engineer that lives like a pet inside <em>your</em> Cloudflare account, on the free or paid plan you already have, instead of another $20–100/seat subscription on someone else's servers.</p>
      <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">I am sharing this work under <a href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba/blob/main/LICENSE" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline">AGPL-3.0-only</a> so improvements to a hosted version remain available to its users too.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE BREAKDOWN GRID -->
  <div class="shell">
    
    <!-- 01: THE PROBLEM -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-problem">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">01 / The Status Quo</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-problem" class="text-[var(--ink)]">The Three Broken Traps of AI Coding Today</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Over the past year, the industry split into three unsatisfactory extremes:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 my-8">
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="card-num">01</div>
            <h3 class="text-[var(--ink)]">The $100/Seat SaaS Trap</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
              Closed platforms demand $20 to $100+ per engineer per month. In return, you surrender your proprietary repositories, internal packages, and secrets to multi-tenant servers outside your security perimeter.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="card-num">02</div>
            <h3 class="text-[var(--ink)]">The Local CLI Bottleneck</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
              Running CLI agents on your personal MacBook is private, but it locks up your terminal, drains your battery, dies when your laptop sleeps, and cannot collaborate with your team via Slack or GitHub webhooks.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="card-num">03</div>
            <h3 class="text-[var(--ink)]">The Heavy DevOps Nightmare</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
              Traditional self-hosting requires AWS EC2, Kubernetes, Docker Compose, Postgres, Redis, and Celery. You end up paying $100+/mo for idle VMs and spending weekends maintaining infrastructure instead of shipping.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          We don't need another proprietary SaaS middleman, and we don't need a fragile server farm. <strong>We need an autonomous coworker that lives in the cloud, runs on serverless infrastructure, and costs nothing when it isn't working.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02: THE CLOUDFLARE FOUNDATION -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-cloudflare">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">02 / The Solution</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-cloudflare" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Why Cloudflare is the Ultimate Substrate for Everyone</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Cloudflare is not just a CDN anymore. It has quietly built the most powerful serverless compute platform on Earth. By building Shiba natively on Cloudflare, anyone can deploy a personal or team software engineer into their own Cloudflare account in under 5 minutes.
        </p>

        <!-- 5 PILLARS -->
        <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
          
          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon">01</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Cloudflare Workers: Sub-Millisecond Orchestrator</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The planning brain runs as an edge Worker using TypeScript and the Cloudflare Agents SDK. It handles incoming Slack mentions, dashboard commands, GitHub webhooks, and cron triggers with zero cold starts and sub-millisecond edge routing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon">02</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Cloudflare Sandbox Containers: Ephemeral Isolated MicroVMs</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every task gets its own dedicated, isolated micro-container running a verified Docker toolchain. It spins up in seconds, clones your branch, runs tests, fixes bugs using headless OpenCode, Claude Code, or Codex, captures unified diffs, and terminates. No persistent compute bill.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row highlight">
            <div class="pillar-icon accent">03</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-2">Cloudflare AI Gateway: The Sacred Security Perimeter</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Your real API keys never enter the container.</strong> Inside the sandbox, the agent CLI uses dummy credentials. All outbound LLM requests are intercepted at egress and authenticated through your account's Cloudflare AI Gateway. Even if untrusted third-party code runs in the sandbox, your provider credentials cannot be leaked.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon">04</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Cloudflare Durable Objects &amp; SQLite: Reliable State &amp; Approvals</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Durable Objects provide atomic coordination: the Human Approval Queue, WebSocket hibernation for real-time log streaming, and session history stored in private embedded SQLite tables. Long-term memory is indexed via Cloudflare Vectorize.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon">05</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust): Enterprise-Grade Security</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No complex JWT/OAuth servers to manage. Cloudflare Access fronts your web dashboard and runner APIs with single sign-on (SSO), email pins, or hardware keys. Machine callers (Slack HMAC, GitHub webhooks) authenticate via cryptographic signatures.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03: CLOUDROOM CORE COMPARISON -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-cloudroom">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">03 / Alternative Runtimes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-cloudroom" class="text-[var(--ink)]">How Shiba Compares to Cloudroom Core</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          There are other thoughtful open-source efforts tackling remote agent execution. For instance, <a href="https://github.com/davidondrej/cloudroom-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">Cloudroom Core</a> is a pre-release Rust service that runs on an Ubuntu Linux VM backed by PostgreSQL.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          While Cloudroom Core focuses on long-lived session continuity, journal replay, and local process management on a persistent server, <strong>Shiba</strong> is designed around an entirely different philosophy:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Cloudroom Core</h4>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>Rust service deployed on a dedicated Linux VM with systemd.</li>
              <li>Requires a separate dedicated PostgreSQL database.</li>
              <li>Focuses on durable multi-turn session journals and conversation replay.</li>
              <li>Harness inference credentials live directly in the agent VM account.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-2">Shiba (Cloudflare Native)</h4>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink)] space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>Serverless Cloudflare Worker with instant edge routing.</li>
              <li>Ephemeral per-task micro-containers (Cloudflare Sandbox SDK).</li>
              <li>Strict Human Approval Gate before any container boots.</li>
              <li>Real API keys stay at Cloudflare AI Gateway egress — never in the sandbox.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04: HOW TO RUN -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-deploy">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">04 / Deployment</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-deploy" class="text-[var(--ink)]">How Anyone Can Deploy Shiba in 4 Commands</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          You don't need a team of DevOps engineers. You can deploy Shiba to your Cloudflare account right now:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] p-5 font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink)] my-6 space-y-4 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 1. Clone the open-source repository</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">git clone</span> https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba.git<br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">cd</span> shiba &amp;&amp; <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">pnpm install</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 2. Configure Cloudflare credentials &amp; optional GitHub token</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">cp</span> .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 3. Test locally with live Sandbox micro-containers</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">pnpm dev</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 4. Deploy production build to your personal or team Cloudflare account</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">pnpm deploy</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-[var(--ink-2)] font-mono">
          For detailed instructions on Slack setup, GitHub App configuration, and Cloudflare Access SSO policies, consult the <a href="/docs/getting-started/" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">Quickstart Documentation</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLOSING CTA -->
    <section class="closing-section">
      <div class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] font-mono text-[var(--navy)] text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] mb-4 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
        BE PART OF THE FUTURE
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] tracking-tight mb-4">
        Help us build the software engineer you would actually trust.
      </h2>
      <p class="lead mb-8 text-[var(--ink-2)]">
        Shiba is open-source under AGPL-3.0-only. Whether you want to test the early access release, contribute code, design, write docs, or follow along—we invite you to join the journey.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions justify-center">
        <a class="button text-sm px-8 py-3.5" href="/waitlist/">
          <span>Join the Waitlist &amp; Contribute →</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button button-outline text-sm px-8 py-3.5" href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <span>Star on GitHub ↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</MarketingPage>

